      "text": "Observability: every evidence record names the ruleset id and hash and each line's rule_id, including the conflict form with its candidates, so a run proves which table and rule categorised each line. The evals summary names the ruleset. No remote alert, by design (design Observability: nothing leaves the machine); the failure that matters, a bad table, fails closed at load with a non-zero exit and no evidence written (test_cma_run_refuses_a_changed_v1_and_writes_no_evidence).",
      "proof_cmd": "pytest tests/test_rules_v2.py -k 'cma_run_under_v2_categorises_with_v2'; pytest tests/test_rules.py -k refuses_a_changed_v1",
      "proof_output": "both pass within the 688 suite run; record['ruleset'] == {'id': 'v2', 'sha256': ...}; per-line rule ids asserted"
    },
    {
      "text": "Operability: the change sits behind the CMA_RULESET flag (default v1). The rollback is CMA_RULESET=v1, exercised by the rollback test in this environment and by the v1 evals run. The only config change is the CMA_RULESET env var; no secrets.",
      "proof_cmd": "CMA_RULESET=v1 python evals/run_evals.py --llm fake; pytest -k rollback_to_v1",
      "proof_output": "9/9 memos fully grounded; ruleset = v1; rollback 1 passed"
    },
    {
      "text": "Docs: the README categoriser row documents the precedence, the ambiguity verdict with both-stage candidates, the rail-plus-cash rule, category_order and whole-word matching in any script, with CMA_RULESET=v1 as the rollback. The decision is recorded in design.md revision 4. The v2.yaml header comments describe the revision 4 verdict.",
      "proof_cmd": "git diff 43f3e57 -- README.md | grep -c 'conflict\\|category_order\\|any script'",
      "proof_output": "README categoriser row contains v2:conflict:, category_order and 'Names match as whole words in any script'"
    }
  ],
  "files_changed": [
    "src/cma/rules/v2.yaml",
    "tests/test_rules_v2.py"
  ],
  "notes": "Hardener change: v2's card_settlement rule (v1's synthetic acquirer wording ACQUIRER SETTLEMENT / PAYGATE PAYOUT / CARD SETTLEMENT) was a bare regex with no word edges, so a Unicode letter glued to it (CAFÉACQUIRER SETTLEMENT) still counted as trading receipts. This is the same class as verify-32 finding 3. It is now a names list (same words, same rule id), with test_p_r4_v1_acquirer_wording_is_whole_words_in_any_script_too. v1 is untouched; v2's hash changes (no signed pack exists).\n\nGaps for the owner (not changed; each is a design or scope call):\n(1) The remaining regex-pattern rules in v2 do not fully follow design C1's 'whole words in any script'. invoice_transfer has a Unicode-aware leading \\b but no trailing edge, so 'INV 12É' or 'INV 123A' still matches; adding an edge would stop letter-suffixed invoice refs from matching, which is a behaviour call. loan_drawdown and loan_repayment ('\\bLOAN\\b|FINANCE\\b') have no leading edge on FINANCE, so 'REFINANCE LTD' is a loan drawdown. That is non-trading and can only lower M2. The debit-only patterns (hmrc_vat, hmrc_paye, owner_drawings, bank_fees, payroll) are v1 wording. A decision is needed on whether C1's whole-word rule covers pattern rules too.\n(2) No remote alerting, by design (local-first); the fail-closed table load stands in for an alert. No runbook entry beyond the README rollback line.\n(3) No changelog file exists in the repo, so no release-note line was written; the README is the user-facing note.\n(4) The judgement queue is R15/T21 per D60 and was deliberately not built here.\n(5) The changes are left uncommitted in the worktree for the pipeline to commit."
}

